#{[17592186180521 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180521 #edb/ref 1061 36179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e668d2a-9da1-5eaf-be19-c24fd0a3b12a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180547 #edb/ref 1061 4285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a309239-eefc-54be-8199-bbf772d5350e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1061 4287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35d16b06-b444-5257-a405-5223a9834174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1064 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification. \n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0.$ Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}.$\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} & = \\\\ |6a| & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |a| \\\\ 6|a| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|a|=a.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6|a| = 6a .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1065 5905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "414812ea-abcc-59df-98fa-8a79ae36a1a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0.$ Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}.$\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{t^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{(t)^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t|t|.&\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0.$ To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives $|t| = -t.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}.\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2.$ Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & = \\\\ -(-2) & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1065 5928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f0207ef-9d6a-584b-8d44-a92c732cce4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1067 "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Negative Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1061 643569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc9d4179-5034-59aa-b0e6-47636130b8db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1064 "The problem of finding the length of a circle or a part of a circle often appears in real life. \n\nFor example, consider the pie in the shape of a circle divided into six equal slices, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/48/48b416b9dffbf6e3e746e3fa8f90ec8043f937fe6